var e=[{id:`rain-shadow-sanctuary`,label:`Rain-Shadow Sanctuary`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Dry and sunny, in the lee of mountains that catch the rain first.`,guide:`Storms drop most of their rain and snow as they climb the windward side of a range. The air that comes down the far side is drier and warms as it sinks, so the cloud breaks up. A rain-shadow town can get a quarter of its wetter neighbour's rain, or less, and far more sun. The usual costs are dry summers, irrigation and, inland, a longer fire season.`},{id:`sky-island-refuge`,label:`Sky-Island Refuge`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Cool, forested high ground standing above hot, dry lowlands.`,guide:`A sky island is an isolated range or plateau that rises 1000 m or more above desert or dry grassland. Air cools with height and the mountain catches passing storms, so the road up runs from scrub through oak into pine. Every 500 m of climb makes summer afternoons about 3°C cooler. Winters at the top bring real frost and some snow.`},{id:`eternal-spring-highland`,label:`Eternal-Spring Highland`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Tropical latitude at high altitude: mild afternoons all year and no real winter.`,guide:`In the tropics the temperature depends on height far more than on season. Mexican highland towns between about 1200 and 2700 m have afternoons of roughly 20–30°C in every month. Nights are cool, and close to freezing at the highest towns in January. The year divides into a dry season and a rainy one from about June to September. April and May, before the rain, are the hottest months.`},{id:`cool-summer-maritime`,label:`Cool-Summer Maritime`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Coasts where a cold sea keeps summer afternoons at about 16–22°C.`,guide:`Wind off cold water holds summer down. July afternoons of 16–22°C are normal while places a short drive inland are 10°C warmer. On the Pacific the same sea keeps winter mild. On the North Atlantic and Lake Superior winters are cold and snowy all the same. Fog, low cloud and wind are common, and warm beach days are few.`},{id:`fog-belt-coast`,label:`Fog-Belt Coast`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Coasts under low cloud and fog for much of the summer.`,guide:`A narrow strip beside cold, upwelling water where low grey cloud is the usual summer sky. Mornings are overcast, afternoons often clear for a few hours, and the heat of the interior never arrives. Fog dripping from needles and leaves adds water through the dry season, which is how coast redwoods get through rainless summers. Step a few kilometres inland, or a few hundred metres uphill, and you are in sunshine.`},{id:`orchard-valley`,label:`Orchard Valley`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Valleys where winter chill, summer sun and sloping ground suit tree fruit.`,guide:`Commercial orchards need three things: enough winter cold for the trees to rest, a warm and sunny summer, and ground where cold air can drain away from the blossom on spring nights. The Okanagan, the Annapolis Valley, Hood River and the Lake Michigan shore meet those needs in different ways, through rain shadow, lake or slope. Where fruit has been grown for generations, the climate has been tested.`},{id:`basin-inversion`,label:`Basin Inversion`,tone:`ice`,blurb:`Enclosed basins where cold air and fog settle for days in winter.`,guide:`Under calm winter high pressure, cold air fills a closed basin and a layer of warmer air sits on top like a lid. The valley stays grey, cold and often smoky while the ridges above are in sunshine. An episode can last a week or more, until a storm or strong wind clears it. Winter air quality is the main thing to check.`},{id:`thermal-belt`,label:`Thermal Belt`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`A band partway up the slope that escapes the frost on the valley floor.`,guide:`On clear, calm nights cold air drains downhill and pools in the valley. The slope above the pool stays in warmer air and can be several degrees milder at dawn. Higher still it turns colder and windier again. Orchards, vineyards and old farmhouses are often found in this band, where the frost-free season can run two to four weeks longer than in the bottomland.`},{id:`lake-moderated`,label:`Lake-Moderated`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`A large lake nearby takes the edge off summer heat and winter cold.`,guide:`Deep water warms and cools slowly. A big lake holds spring back, which keeps fruit buds closed until the frosts are over, and it gives off heat through autumn, which delays the first freeze. On the downwind shore peaches and wine grapes survive where they would fail a short drive inland. The effect weakens quickly with distance from the water.`},{id:`lake-effect-snowbelt`,label:`Lake-Effect Snowbelt`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Shores downwind of a large lake that take very heavy snow.`,guide:`When cold air crosses open, unfrozen water it picks up heat and moisture and unloads it as snow on the downwind shore, often in bands only a few kilometres wide. Totals of 2.5 to 7 m a year are normal in these belts. The snow is heaviest from November to January and eases if the lake freezes. Skies are grey for much of the winter.`},{id:`chinook-corridor`,label:`Chinook Corridor`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`East of the mountains, where warm downslope winds break up the winter.`,guide:`Air that crosses the Rockies and sinks down the eastern slope warms as it descends and arrives dry and fast. A chinook can raise the temperature 20–30°C in a few hours and remove the snow cover in a day. Winter comes in spells of deep cold with thaws between them. The same wind dries the land and can reach damaging speeds.`},{id:`high-desert-escape`,label:`High-Desert Escape`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Desert at altitude: dry air, warm days, cool nights.`,guide:`Above about 1500 m the desert loses its worst heat. Afternoons are warm, the air is dry, and nights cool by 15°C or more because dry air under a clear sky holds little warmth. Winters are sunny, with frosty nights. The limits are water, strong sun and, in many places, distance from services.`},{id:`canyon-sheltered`,label:`Canyon-Sheltered`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Towns set between canyon walls, out of the wind and partly shaded.`,guide:`Canyon walls block the wind and shade the floor for part of the day. Cool air flows down-canyon at night and up-canyon breezes rise in the afternoon. A canyon town is usually calmer than the plateau above it, and water and deeper soil collect on the floor. Flash floods and rockfall are the hazards that come with the setting.`},{id:`coastal-upwelling`,label:`Coastal-Upwelling Belt`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Coasts cooled by cold water rising from the deep.`,guide:`In spring and summer, winds blowing along the coast push the surface water out to sea and cold water rises to replace it. The sea can be 10–13°C in July. The shore stays cool and often foggy while the land behind it is hot. The same cold, nutrient-rich water supports kelp forests, seabirds and fisheries.`},{id:`monsoon-edge`,label:`Monsoon-Edge Zone`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Where the summer monsoon arrives, though not equally every year.`,guide:`The North American Monsoon brings afternoon thunderstorms to northwest Mexico and the US Southwest from July to September. Towns near its edge have a hot, dry spring, then a sudden turn to towering clouds, short downpours and green hills. How much rain arrives varies widely from year to year, and flash floods come with the storms.`},{id:`humidity-refuge`,label:`Humidity Refuge`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Places where dry air, altitude or a cool sea keeps summer humidity low.`,guide:`Humidity decides how heat feels. Where the summer dew point stays around 10–15°C, sweat evaporates and nights cool off. Where it sits at 22–24°C, as on the Gulf Coast, the same air temperature is far more tiring and nights stay warm. These places share the first kind of summer.`},{id:`mild-winter-foothills`,label:`Mild-Winter Foothills`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Low hills at the foot of the mountains that miss the worst winter cold.`,guide:`Foothills sit above the cold air that pools on the valley floor and below the wind and snow of the high country. Winter nights are milder, snow melts sooner and spring starts earlier than in either neighbour. Many old farms and orchards were sited on these slopes for that reason.`},{id:`mediterranean-pocket`,label:`Mediterranean Pocket`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Mild, wet winters and dry, sunny summers.`,guide:`The Mediterranean pattern, with rain in winter and almost none in summer, occurs in only five regions of the world. In North America it runs from northern Baja California through California, and a cooler version reaches the rain shadows of Oregon, Washington and southern British Columbia. Hills are green in winter and brown by July. Olives, grapes and stone fruit do well, and summer irrigation and wildfire are the costs.`},{id:`cloud-forest`,label:`Cloud Forest`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Mountain forest that is often inside the cloud and takes water from it.`,guide:`On slopes that face a moist wind, cloud forms at a steady height and the forest there stands in mist for much of the year. Leaves and moss collect droplets and drip them to the ground, which adds to an already heavy rainfall. Temperature and humidity change little from day to day. Expect mud, moss, tree ferns and very few dry weeks.`},{id:`volcanic-upland`,label:`Volcanic Upland`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`High country on lava and ash soils.`,guide:`Weathered volcanic ash makes deep, loose, fertile soil that holds water well and still drains. Young lava and cinder are the opposite: thin, rocky and quick to dry. At altitude in the tropics the ash soils carry coffee, avocado and maize. Farther north they carry pine forest. Local crops are a fair guide to which kind of ground you are standing on.`},{id:`limestone-karst`,label:`Limestone / Karst Pocket`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Limestone country where water sinks underground and soils are thin.`,guide:`In karst, rain drains through cracks in the limestone into caves and underground streams, and comes back to the surface at springs. There are few creeks. Soils are shallow, alkaline and stony, and gardens dry out fast. Sinkholes and cave mouths stay cool and damp. Wells, springs and cisterns matter more here than rainfall totals suggest.`},{id:`river-valley-moderation`,label:`River-Valley Moderation`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Large river valleys that carry milder air inland.`,guide:`A big river valley is a low route through high ground. Sea air moves up it, and cold or hot air drains along it, so the valley floor is often milder than the uplands on either side. The Columbia and the St. Lawrence carry this effect hundreds of kilometres from the coast. Valley floors also have deep river soils, and fog and flood risk.`},{id:`urban-heat-contrast`,label:`Urban Heat Contrast`,tone:`ember`,blurb:`City centres that run several degrees warmer than their surroundings, mainly at night.`,guide:`Pavement and buildings store the day's heat and release it after dark. That delays frost and lengthens the growing season in the city, and it makes summer nights hotter and heat waves harder to bear. Tall streets can also channel wind or trap still air. Parks, rivers and the leafy edge of town can be noticeably cooler than the core.`},{id:`desert-oasis`,label:`Desert Oasis`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Springs, rivers or shallow groundwater supporting green ground in the desert.`,guide:`A dependable source of water changes a small area of desert. Trees give shade, the air is slightly more humid, and birds and people gather there. Oases are fragile, because many draw on old groundwater that is refilled slowly or not at all. The condition of the spring or aquifer matters as much as the climate.`},{id:`hyper-maritime`,label:`Hyper-Maritime`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Outer coasts where the ocean leaves little difference between the seasons.`,guide:`On exposed headlands and outer islands, summer afternoons reach only 16–20°C and winter afternoons mostly stay at 5–10°C. Hard frost and hot days are both uncommon. Cloud, wind, salt spray and long spells of rain or fog shape daily life. This is the most ocean-controlled climate in North America.`},{id:`driftless-relief`,label:`Driftless Relief Pocket`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Hill country the glaciers missed, with steep valleys and varied slopes.`,guide:`The Driftless Area of the upper Mississippi was never flattened by ice, so it kept its ridges, bluffs and narrow valleys. Within a few kilometres there are warm south-facing slopes, cold valley floors, and north-facing rock slopes that stay cool all summer. What grows, and when the frost comes, depends on exactly where you stand.`},{id:`piedmont-transition`,label:`Piedmont Transition`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Rolling country between the mountains and the plain.`,guide:`A piedmont is the belt of low hills at the foot of a mountain range. It is a little cooler than the plain below, less steep and less snowy than the mountains above, and well supplied with streams. Many piedmonts are farming and wine country. Climate changes gradually across them, so two sites an hour apart can differ by a few degrees.`},{id:`cold-air-pool`,label:`Cold-Air Pool`,tone:`ice`,blurb:`Valleys where cold air settles at night and the floor runs colder than the slopes above.`,guide:`On calm, clear nights cold air drains off the slopes and fills an enclosed valley from the bottom. In winter a lid of warmer air can hold it there for days or weeks. The floor can be more than 10°C colder than slopes a few hundred metres higher. Peter Sinks, a basin in the mountains above Logan, has recorded −56°C, the second-lowest reading in the contiguous United States.`},{id:`frost-hollow`,label:`Frost Hollow`,tone:`ice`,blurb:`Small dips in the land where frost comes later in spring and earlier in autumn.`,guide:`A frost hollow is any low spot where cold air collects on still nights. It need not be a deep valley; a shallow bowl in a field is enough. The frost-free season there can be weeks shorter than on the slope beside it. Gardeners find these spots by losing tomatoes to a June frost. They range from a few metres to several kilometres across.`},{id:`santa-ana-corridor`,label:`Santa Ana / Sundowner Corridor`,tone:`ember`,blurb:`Canyons and passes where hot, dry wind blows from the interior toward the coast.`,guide:`When high pressure builds inland, air pours through the passes and canyons toward the sea, warming and drying as it descends. Santa Anas in southern California, sundowners at Santa Barbara and Diablo winds around San Francisco Bay all work this way. They bring sudden heat, very low humidity and clear views, mostly in autumn, and many of California's most destructive wildfires have burned under them.`},{id:`gap-wind-corridor`,label:`Gap / Gorge Wind Corridor`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Gaps in the mountains where wind is funnelled and strengthened.`,guide:`Where a river or pass cuts through a range, air moves through the opening from the high-pressure side to the low. The Columbia River Gorge, Cajon Pass, Tehachapi Pass and the Strait of Juan de Fuca are examples. The wind is steady and comes from a predictable direction, which suits windsurfers and wind farms. A gap is also where the weather of one region meets the next.`},{id:`fjord-inlet`,label:`Fjord / Inlet Coast`,tone:`glacier`,blurb:`Deep, steep-sided inlets where sea and mountain climates meet.`,guide:`A fjord brings sea water far into the mountains. The outer coast is mild, wet and windy. Toward the head of the inlet, winters are colder and summers warmer, and cold air draining off inland snowfields can blow down the fjord for days. One wall may be in shade for much of the winter. Rainfall changes sharply along the length of the inlet.`},{id:`alpine-tundra`,label:`Alpine Tundra`,tone:`ice`,blurb:`High mountain towns and summits near or above treeline, with short, cool summers.`,guide:`Near treeline the growing season is a matter of weeks and frost is possible in any month. Snow lies from autumn well into spring, the air is thin, and the sun is strong. Summer nights are often 15–20°C cooler than the afternoons. People live at these heights year-round, but altitude affects sleep, exertion and health, and takes time to adjust to.`},{id:`subarctic-continental`,label:`Subarctic Continental`,tone:`aurora`,blurb:`The northern interior: winters that reach −40°C and short summers that reach 25°C.`,guide:`Far from the ocean and north of about 58°, the difference between winter and summer is as large as anywhere on Earth outside Siberia. Whitehorse, Yellowknife and Fairbanks have long, dark, dry-cold winters and short summers with daylight most of the night. Spruce forest, muskeg and permafrost cover the land. Day length shapes the year as much as temperature does.`},{id:`tropical-isothermal`,label:`Tropical Isothermal Coast`,tone:`sage`,blurb:`Tropical coasts where every month is nearly as warm as every other.`,guide:`On tropical coasts the sea and the high sun keep monthly temperatures within a few degrees of each other all year. Seasons are marked by rain: a dry season in winter and spring, and a wet season from about June to October. Nights stay warm and the air is humid. The cooler, drier months are the comfortable ones.`},{id:`tropical-dry`,label:`Tropical Wet-Dry`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Tropical lowlands with a long dry season and a summer rainy season.`,guide:`The tropical wet-and-dry climate (Köppen Aw) of the Yucatán, the Oaxaca coast and the lowlands of western Mexico. Almost no rain falls from November to May, and forest and fields turn brown. The rains arrive in June and within weeks everything is green. Farming, building and festivals all follow that calendar.`},{id:`hurricane-coast`,label:`Hurricane-Exposed Coast`,tone:`ember`,blurb:`Warm coasts where hurricanes and tropical storms make landfall.`,guide:`On the Gulf of Mexico, the Caribbean and the US Atlantic coast, tropical cyclones are part of the climate. They shape building codes, insurance costs and the vegetation, and they make late summer and autumn a watchful season. Some stretches are hit every few years. Others go decades between major storms and then take a severe one.`},{id:`badland-steppe`,label:`Badland / Steppe Pocket`,tone:`ochre`,blurb:`Dry, eroded country at the edge of the Great Plains.`,guide:`In badlands and dry breaks such as those of the Dakotas and southern Alberta, bare, steep, deeply cut ground heats fast by day and cools fast at night. Which way a slope faces matters a great deal: south-facing slopes are hot and nearly bare, while north-facing draws hold snow longer and grow shrubs and trees. Wind is constant and shade is scarce.`}],t=Object.fromEntries(e.map(e=>[e.id,e])),n=[{id:`rain-shadows`,title:`Rain-Shadow Sanctuaries`,subtitle:`The dry, sunny side of a wet mountain range`,description:`Towns in the lee of the Olympics, the Cascades, the Coast Mountains and the Sierra Nevada, where the storms have already dropped their rain on the far slope. Sequim gets about 430 mm of rain a year; the Hoh valley, 65 km away across the Olympics, gets about 4000 mm. Sixteen of these 25 places get under 500 mm. Expect more sun and drier air than the region is known for. The costs are summer drought, irrigation and, inland, wildfire smoke.`,tone:`ochre`,placeIds:[`sequim-wa`,`port-townsend-wa`,`osoyoos-bc`,`bishop-ca`,`victoria-bc`,`hood-river-or`,`hood-river-gorge`,`ashland-or`,`sunshine-coast-bc`,`salt-spring-bc`,`summerland-bc`,`wenatchee-wa`,`ellensburg-wa`,`kamloops-bc`,`qualicum-bc`,`whitehorse-yt`,`medford-or`,`winthrop-wa`,`leavenworth-wa`,`lone-pine-ca`,`kelowna-bc`,`page-az`,`friday-harbor-wa`,`palm-springs-ca`,`spokane-wa`]},{id:`sky-islands`,title:`Sky-Island Refuges`,subtitle:`Cool, forested high ground above hot lowlands`,description:`Isolated mountains and plateaus that stand 1,000 m or more above the country around them and are cooler and wetter for it. Most are in the desert Southwest and northern Mexico, where pine and oak grow within sight of cactus flats. Cypress Hills above the Saskatchewan prairie and Mount Washington above the New England forest are the same idea in other settings. They offer summer relief within a short drive of hot basins, and real winter at the top.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`portal-az`,`silver-city-nm`,`creel-mx`,`flagstaff-az`,`santa-fe-nm`,`fort-davis-tx`,`cloudcroft-nm`,`cypress-hills-sk`,`real-catorce-mx`,`mount-washington-nh`,`mount-charleston-nv`]},{id:`cold-air-pools`,title:`Cold-Air Pools & Inversion Basins`,subtitle:`Valley floors that run colder than the slopes above them`,description:`Basins and high valleys where cold air drains off the slopes on clear, calm nights and settles on the floor. Winter nights average −19°C in Gunnison and −18°C in Stanley, far colder than their latitude suggests, and in midwinter an inversion can hold fog and smoke in the valley for days. Summer brings the other half of the pattern: warm afternoons, and nights 18–23°C cooler in the dry western basins. Useful if you want cold, reliable winters, and a warning if you garden.`,tone:`ice`,placeIds:[`gunnison-co`,`logan-ut`,`canaan-valley-wv`,`missoula-mt`,`truckee-ca`,`crested-butte-co`,`stanley-id`,`fairbanks-ak`,`international-falls-mn`,`winthrop-wa`,`morgantown-wv`,`burkes-garden-va`]},{id:`eternal-spring`,title:`Eternal-Spring Highlands`,subtitle:`Tropical latitude, cooled by altitude`,description:`Mexican highland towns between about 1200 and 2700 m. Altitude takes the heat out of a tropical latitude, so winter afternoons run 19–29°C and the warmest and coldest months are only 4–8°C apart. The seasons are wet and dry: most rain falls June to September, and the hottest weather comes in April and May, just before it, with afternoons of 25–33°C. Winter nights are cool, 2–11°C, and few houses are heated.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`oaxaca-mx`,`cuernavaca-mx`,`san-cristobal-mx`,`patzcuaro-mx`,`ajijic-lake-chapala-mx`,`san-miguel-de-allende-mx`,`queretaro-mx`,`orizaba-mx`,`tapalpa-mx`,`mazamitla-mx`,`morelia-mx`,`guanajuato-mx`,`zacatecas-mx`,`toluca-mx`,`taxco-mx`,`puebla-mx`]},{id:`cool-summer-maritimes`,title:`Cool-Summer Maritimes`,subtitle:`Coasts where the sea keeps summer cool`,description:`Shores of the Pacific, the North Atlantic, the Gulf of St. Lawrence and Lake Superior where cold water holds summer down. At most of these places summer afternoons average 16–23°C, and at Prince Rupert and Sitka only 16°C. A few at the warm end, such as Cape May and Burlington, reach 26–29°C but stay cooler than the country inland. These are the places to look at if you want to get away from heat. Fog, wind and a short swimming season come with them.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:`monterey-ca.santa-cruz-felton-ca.tofino-bc.victoria-bc.grand-marais-mn.grand-manan-nb.baie-saint-paul-qc.eureka-ca.point-reyes-ca.astoria-or.forks-wa.prince-rupert-bc.tofino-ucluelet-corridor.gaspe-qc.st-johns-nl.cannon-beach-or.sitka-ak.twillingate-nl.ensenada-mx.duluth-mn.bar-harbor-me.halifax-ns.charlottetown-pei.squamish-bc.friday-harbor-wa.brookings-or.burlington-vt.block-island-ri.cape-may-nj`.split(`.`)},{id:`orchard-valleys`,title:`Fruit & Orchard Valleys`,subtitle:`Where apples, cherries, peaches and grapes ripen`,description:`Valleys and lakeshores where commercial fruit growing shows what the local climate can do. A lake delays spring frost in Traverse City and Niagara. A rain shadow gives the Okanagan and Wenatchee dry, sunny summers. Sloping ground lets cold air drain away from the trees at Hood River. Worth reading if you garden or farm, since each one shows how terrain makes a growing season.`,tone:`ochre`,placeIds:[`hood-river-or`,`osoyoos-bc`,`traverse-city-mi`,`niagara-on-the-lake`,`wolfville-ns`,`baie-saint-paul-qc`,`morden-mb`,`sutton-qc`,`ashland-or`,`summerland-bc`,`creston-bc`,`prince-edward-co-on`,`wenatchee-wa`,`penticton-bc`,`leamington-on`,`cuauhtemoc-mx`,`medford-or`,`kelowna-bc`,`leavenworth-wa`,`napa-ca`]},{id:`thermal-belts`,title:`Thermal Belts & Frost Refuges`,subtitle:`Slopes that stay above the valley frost`,description:`Hillside towns that sit above the pool of cold air on the valley floor on clear nights. Spring frost ends earlier and autumn frost comes later than in the bottomland nearby, which is why orchards and old farmhouses are found on these slopes. The effect is local: it can change within a few hundred metres up or down the hill, so check the exact site.`,tone:`ochre`,placeIds:[`black-mountain-nc`,`hood-river-or`,`ashland-or`,`sedona-az`,`viroqua-wi`,`gatlinburg-tn`]},{id:`chinook-country`,title:`Chinook & Downslope Wind Corridors`,subtitle:`Winter thaws on a warm mountain wind`,description:`Towns east of the Rockies, the Cascades and the Black Hills, where air coming down off the mountains arrives warm and dry. Winter nights still average −4 to −15°C here. What differs is the breaks: a chinook can lift the temperature 20°C in a few hours and clear the snow in a day. The same winds are strong enough to close highways, and they dry out grass and timber in fire season.`,tone:`ochre`,placeIds:[`lethbridge-ab`,`boulder-co`,`cody-wy`,`pincher-creek-ab`,`bozeman-mt`,`medicine-hat-ab`,`banff-ab`,`leavenworth-wa`,`bismarck-nd`,`spearfish-sd`]},{id:`gap-winds`,title:`Gap & Gorge Wind Corridors`,subtitle:`Where the wind funnels through the mountains`,description:`Gorges, straits and passes that carry air from one side of a range to the other: the Columbia River Gorge, the Strait of Juan de Fuca, Crowsnest Pass, the Isthmus of Tehuantepec. Mount Washington is here for its exposed summit. The wind blows from the same quarter for days at a time, which is good for windsurfing and wind farms and hard on gardens, roofs and nerves.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:[`hood-river-gorge`,`ellensburg-wa`,`port-townsend-wa`,`pincher-creek-ab`,`mount-washington-nh`,`la-ventosa-mx`,`brookings-or`,`squamish-bc`,`chattanooga-tn`]},{id:`snowbelts`,title:`Lake-Effect & Mountain Snowbelts`,subtitle:`From 240 cm to more than 900 cm of snow a year`,description:`Shores downwind of the Great Lakes and mountain slopes that face the storm track. Station records give Geneva-on-the-Lake about 240 cm of snow a year, Syracuse 380 cm, Houghton 550 cm, Redfield on the Tug Hill Plateau 700 cm and Valdez more than 900 cm. The snow is dependable, which is the attraction for skiers. Roofs, driveways and winter driving are the price.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:[`grand-marais-mi`,`redfield-ny`,`syracuse-ny`,`marquette-mi`,`houghton-mi`,`revelstoke-bc`,`mammoth-lakes-ca`,`valdez-ak`,`erie-pa`,`duluth-mn`,`geneva-on-the-lake-oh`]},{id:`hidden-gems`,title:`Lesser-Known Local Climates`,subtitle:`Distinctive climates in places few people shortlist`,description:`Small towns and out-of-the-way districts with a strongly local climate and a low profile. Many are far from a large airport or a hospital, and that distance is part of why they are little known. Use this set to widen a search beyond the usual names, and read each profile's Cost and access section before going further.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:`portal-az.silver-city-nm.grand-marais-mn.grand-manan-nb.creel-mx.baie-saint-paul-qc.dawson-city-yt.sutton-qc.durango-mx.orizaba-mx.tapalpa-mx.xilitla-mx.alamos-mx.salt-spring-bc.sunshine-coast-bc.creston-bc.mazamitla-mx.canaan-valley-wv.fort-davis-tx.cypress-hills-sk.joseph-or.real-catorce-mx.parras-de-la-fuente-mx.cuauhtemoc-mx.redfield-ny.houghton-mi.viroqua-wi.stanley-id.twillingate-nl.ely-mn.xalapa-mx.haida-gwaii-bc.medicine-hat-ab.winthrop-wa.mount-charleston-nv.burkes-garden-va`.split(`.`)},{id:`alpine-tundra`,title:`High-Altitude Towns`,subtitle:`Life between 1800 m and 3100 m`,description:`Towns high enough for thin air and strong sun to shape daily life. Leadville, at 3094 m, has air pressure about 70% of that at sea level. In Colorado and the Sierra Nevada the frost-free season is only 74–105 days and winter nights average −8 to −20°C. The Mexican members are different: Toluca and Real de Catorce sit just as high but have mild winters and can go most of the year without frost. Mount Washington's summit is the one true alpine-tundra site.`,tone:`ice`,placeIds:[`leadville-co`,`crested-butte-co`,`mammoth-lakes-ca`,`truckee-ca`,`cloudcroft-nm`,`real-catorce-mx`,`toluca-mx`,`mount-washington-nh`,`creel-mx`]},{id:`cloud-forests`,title:`Cloud Forests & Wet Mountain Slopes`,subtitle:`Forests that take water from cloud as well as rain`,description:`Wet slopes facing the wind, where cloud sits on the forest for much of the year. Every place here gets more than 1350 mm of rain a year and Hilo gets about 3500 mm. Xalapa, Coatepec and Orizaba are Mexican cloud-forest country. Highlands, Boone and Gatlinburg are in the wet southern Appalachians, where Highlands gets about 2300 mm. Hilo and Palenque are lowland rainforest, included for comparison. Expect green in every month, moss, mud and few fully clear days.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`highlands-nc`,`xilitla-mx`,`coatepec-mx`,`san-cristobal-mx`,`orizaba-mx`,`boone-nc`,`xalapa-mx`,`gatlinburg-tn`,`palenque-mx`,`hilo-hi`]},{id:`dry-air-comfort`,title:`Low-Humidity Summers`,subtitle:`Dry air and cool nights`,description:`Places where summer air is dry enough for sweat to evaporate and for nights to cool off. Average summer humidity is 22–24% in Bishop, Lone Pine and Page, and across the set summer nights are 14–23°C cooler than the afternoons. Several are hot by day: Hermosillo and Borrego Springs average 39°C in summer. The Mexican members turn more humid when the rains arrive in July. Look here if humid heat is what you want to avoid.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:[`bishop-ca`,`silver-city-nm`,`santa-fe-nm`,`flagstaff-az`,`boulder-co`,`creel-mx`,`san-miguel-de-allende-mx`,`queretaro-mx`,`durango-mx`,`taos-nm`,`prescott-az`,`borrego-springs-ca`,`marfa-tx`,`lubbock-tx`,`page-az`,`hermosillo-mx`,`lone-pine-ca`]},{id:`monsoon-edges`,title:`Monsoon-Edge Landscapes`,subtitle:`Summer thunderstorms after a dry spring`,description:`Places reached by the North American Monsoon. Between a third and three-quarters of the year's rain falls from July to September, most of it in afternoon thunderstorms. May and June are hot and dry, and the hills turn green within weeks of the first storms. Totals are modest, 170–820 mm of rain a year, and vary a good deal from one summer to the next.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`silver-city-nm`,`portal-az`,`flagstaff-az`,`alamos-mx`,`santa-fe-nm`,`fort-davis-tx`,`marfa-tx`,`cloudcroft-nm`,`sedona-az`,`durango-co`,`durango-mx`,`taos-nm`,`page-az`,`monterrey-mx`,`hermosillo-mx`,`puebla-mx`,`tucson-az`]},{id:`hyper-maritime`,title:`Hyper-Maritime Coasts`,subtitle:`Outer coasts with little difference between seasons`,description:`Exposed coasts and islands where the ocean sets the temperature all year. On the Pacific, from Eureka to Sitka, the warmest and coldest months are only 6–12°C apart and summer afternoons stay at 16–20°C. Grand Manan, Twillingate and Block Island on the Atlantic have colder winters but the same wind, fog and cool summers. Rain is heavy on the northern Pacific coast: Tofino gets about 3000 mm of rain a year.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:[`tofino-bc`,`grand-manan-nb`,`forks-wa`,`eureka-ca`,`prince-rupert-bc`,`tofino-ucluelet-corridor`,`astoria-or`,`sitka-ak`,`haida-gwaii-bc`,`cannon-beach-or`,`twillingate-nl`,`brookings-or`,`block-island-ri`]},{id:`subarctic`,title:`Far North & Deep Winter`,subtitle:`Long, severe winters and short, bright summers`,description:`The coldest winters in the atlas. Winter nights average −17 to −28°C at ten of these eleven places; Anchorage, on Cook Inlet, is milder at about −11°C. Summers are short, with 86–148 frost-free days, and afternoons of about 20°C in the interior and 10–16°C on the Arctic, Bering and Hudson Bay coasts. Ely and Thunder Bay stand in for the same winter farther south. Daylight runs to extremes in both directions north of 60°.`,tone:`ice`,placeIds:[`whitehorse-yt`,`dawson-city-yt`,`yellowknife-nt`,`churchill-mb`,`thunder-bay-on`,`fairbanks-ak`,`nome-ak`,`iqaluit-nu`,`inuvik-nt`,`ely-mn`,`anchorage-ak`]},{id:`desert-oases`,title:`Desert Oases`,subtitle:`Desert towns that depend on a spring, a river or an aquifer`,description:`Settlements in very dry country that exist because of a dependable water source: the springs at Parras and Furnace Creek, the Colorado River at Yuma and Moab, groundwater at Borrego Springs and La Paz. Rain totals 54–400 mm a year and summer afternoons average 31–46°C. Winters are the reason to come. Look into the water supply as carefully as the weather.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`parras-de-la-fuente-mx`,`moab-ut`,`borrego-springs-ca`,`alamos-mx`,`la-paz-mx`,`death-valley-ca`,`yuma-az`]},{id:`tropical-coasts`,title:`Tropical & Hurricane Coasts`,subtitle:`Warm coasts in the path of tropical storms`,description:`Coasts of the Gulf of Mexico, the Caribbean, the Mexican Pacific, Hawai‘i and the US Southeast. On the tropical ones (Puerto Escondido, Bacalar, Hilo, Honolulu) the warmest and coldest months differ by only 3–4°C and the year divides into wet and dry. The northern Gulf and Carolina coasts have a real winter, with nights of 4–10°C. All of them carry hurricane or tropical-storm risk, mostly from June to November, and that risk matters more than the averages.`,tone:`ember`,placeIds:[`key-west-fl`,`charleston-sc`,`apalachicola-fl`,`merida-mx`,`bacalar-mx`,`puerto-escondido-mx`,`la-paz-mx`,`south-padre-tx`,`la-ventosa-mx`,`nags-head-nc`,`new-orleans-la`,`campeche-mx`,`puerto-vallarta-mx`,`mazatlan-mx`,`hilo-hi`,`honolulu-hi`,`corpus-christi-tx`,`ocean-springs-ms`]},{id:`atlantic-maritimes`,title:`Atlantic Canada & Downeast`,subtitle:`Fog, ice and cool summers on the North Atlantic`,description:`From the Bay of Fundy, which has the highest tides in the world, to the iceberg coast of northern Newfoundland. The cold Labrador Current and the Gulf of St. Lawrence keep summer afternoons at 19–24°C. Winters are snowy and stormy, with nights of −5 to −14°C. Spring comes late and autumn is the most settled season.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:[`grand-manan-nb`,`wolfville-ns`,`halifax-ns`,`charlottetown-pei`,`gaspe-qc`,`st-johns-nl`,`twillingate-nl`,`bar-harbor-me`]},{id:`great-lakes-microclimates`,title:`Great Lakes Shores`,subtitle:`Lake-effect snow, cool shores and fruit belts`,description:`Towns whose weather is made by a large lake. Cold air crossing open water buries Houghton, Marquette and Buffalo in snow. Lake Superior keeps Duluth and Grand Marais cool in June. Lakes Erie, Ontario and Michigan delay the frosts that would otherwise rule out peaches and wine grapes at Leamington, Niagara and Traverse City. Burlington, on Lake Champlain, shows the same effects on a smaller lake.`,tone:`ice`,placeIds:[`erie-pa`,`duluth-mn`,`syracuse-ny`,`grand-marais-mi`,`marquette-mi`,`houghton-mi`,`traverse-city-mi`,`thunder-bay-on`,`burlington-vt`,`leamington-on`,`niagara-on-the-lake`,`geneva-on-the-lake-oh`,`buffalo-ny`]},{id:`appalachian-arc`,title:`Appalachian Arc & Eastern Highlands`,subtitle:`Ridges, coves and high valleys of the East`,description:`Mountain towns from Quebec's Eastern Townships down the Blue Ridge to Alabama, with Eureka Springs in the Ozarks. Height takes several degrees off the lowland summer, the ridges wring out heavy rain, and enclosed valleys such as Burkes Garden collect cold air at night. Summers are cooler and greener than on the plains and Piedmont nearby. Heavy-rain floods are the main hazard.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`black-mountain-nc`,`asheville-nc`,`boone-nc`,`highlands-nc`,`sutton-qc`,`gatlinburg-tn`,`chattanooga-tn`,`eureka-springs-ar`,`morgantown-wv`,`mentone-al`,`clayton-ga`,`burkes-garden-va`]},{id:`comfort-exemplars`,title:`Year-Round Comfort: A Short List`,subtitle:`Thirteen places with mild weather in most months`,description:`One or two examples of each kind of place where the weather stays moderate for most of the year: sky islands above the Arizona desert, Mexican highland towns, the cool dry-summer coast of California and Baja California, and the southern Appalachian slopes. None is perfect. The sky islands and the Appalachian towns have frosty winter nights, and the Baja coast gets only 200–240 mm of rain a year. Start here, then follow each profile to similar places.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`huachuca-az`,`portal-az`,`silver-city-nm`,`oaxaca-mx`,`cuernavaca-mx`,`ajijic-lake-chapala-mx`,`san-miguel-de-allende-mx`,`ensenada-mx`,`valle-guadalupe-mx`,`santa-barbara-ca`,`monterey-ca`,`black-mountain-nc`,`highlands-nc`]}],r=Object.fromEntries(n.map(e=>[e.id,e])),i=[{id:`places-that-feel-like-another-country`,title:`Places That Feel Like Another Country`,subtitle:`Local climates unlike the region around them`,description:`Eight stops where the climate is out of character for its region: sagebrush and vineyards in the south Okanagan, a cool pine highland in Chiapas, jungle at Xilitla on the wet face of the Sierra Madre Oriental, spring-fed pools in the Coahuila desert, a rainforest city in Hawai‘i, and the fog and wind of St. John's. Each one is easiest to appreciate beside a more typical neighbour an hour or two away.`,tone:`aurora`,placeIds:[`osoyoos-bc`,`penticton-bc`,`san-cristobal-mx`,`xilitla-mx`,`bacalar-mx`,`cuatrocienegas-mx`,`hilo-hi`,`st-johns-nl`],bestFor:[`strong contrasts`,`a first look at the atlas`,`photography`],seasonHint:`No single season suits the whole list. Check the best months for each stop.`,monetizationHint:`Editorial list only. No packages, paid placement or bookings.`},{id:`sky-island-road-trip`,title:`Sky-Island Road Trip`,subtitle:`From desert basins up to cool forest`,description:`A route planned by elevation. Each stop is a mountain range or high plateau that is cooler and wetter than the basin below it: oak and pine above the Sonoran and Chihuahuan deserts, bristlecone pine above Las Vegas, lodgepole pine above the Saskatchewan prairie. Drive from the valley floor to the top and watch where the vegetation changes.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`portal-az`,`huachuca-az`,`silver-city-nm`,`cloudcroft-nm`,`fort-davis-tx`,`creel-mx`,`cypress-hills-sk`,`mount-charleston-nv`],archetypeFilters:[`sky-island-refuge`,`high-desert-escape`,`monsoon-edge`],bestFor:[`changes with elevation`,`summer thunderstorms`,`cool nights near hot basins`],seasonHint:`Spring and early autumn are easiest: the basins are not at peak heat and the high roads are clear of snow. July and August add afternoon thunderstorms.`,monetizationHint:`Editorial route only. Not a guided trip or a paid itinerary.`},{id:`fog-belt-summer-escape`,title:`Fog-Coast Summers`,subtitle:`Cool coasts next to hot interiors`,description:`Coasts and islands where cold water keeps summer afternoons at about 17–24°C. On the Pacific stops, drive 40 km inland to feel the change: the fog ends and the afternoon can be 10°C warmer or more. The Atlantic stops add wind, and icebergs off Twillingate into early summer.`,tone:`glacier`,placeIds:[`monterey-ca`,`eureka-ca`,`point-reyes-ca`,`cannon-beach-or`,`tofino-bc`,`grand-manan-nb`,`st-johns-nl`,`twillingate-nl`],archetypeFilters:[`fog-belt-coast`,`coastal-upwelling`,`hyper-maritime`,`cool-summer-maritime`],bestFor:[`cool summers`,`fog and low cloud`,`coastal walking`],seasonHint:`July to September, when the contrast with inland heat is greatest. Mornings are often grey and afternoons clearer.`},{id:`rain-shadow-weekend`,title:`Rain-Shadow Weekend`,subtitle:`Dry towns behind wet mountains`,description:`Sunny, dry towns on the lee side of the Olympics, the Cascades, the Coast Mountains and the Sierra Nevada. The year's rain runs from about 130 mm at Bishop to 830 mm at Hood River, a fraction of what falls on the windward slopes. The trip works best when you cross the range and see both sides on the same day.`,tone:`ochre`,placeIds:[`sequim-wa`,`port-townsend-wa`,`victoria-bc`,`osoyoos-bc`,`bishop-ca`,`hood-river-or`,`ellensburg-wa`,`wenatchee-wa`],archetypeFilters:[`rain-shadow-sanctuary`],bestFor:[`sunshine near a wet coast`,`crossing a mountain barrier`,`comparing neighbours`],seasonHint:`Late spring to early autumn shows the dry side most clearly. In winter the contrast with the wet side is largest, but the passes can be snowy.`},{id:`high-desert-wine-and-orchards`,title:`Dry-Summer Wine and Orchard Valleys`,subtitle:`What the fruit says about the climate`,description:`Valleys where hot, dry summer days, cool nights and irrigation water ripen wine grapes and tree fruit: the Okanagan, Wenatchee, Hood River, Napa, Valle de Guadalupe and Parras. Summer rain is slight everywhere except Parras, so growers depend on rivers, lakes or wells. Crops are evidence of a climate, but one orchard does not prove the next field will grow fruit. Frost pockets and water rights change from site to site.`,tone:`ochre`,placeIds:[`valle-guadalupe-mx`,`parras-de-la-fuente-mx`,`osoyoos-bc`,`penticton-bc`,`summerland-bc`,`wenatchee-wa`,`hood-river-or`,`napa-ca`],archetypeFilters:[`orchard-valley`,`rain-shadow-sanctuary`,`mediterranean-pocket`,`high-desert-escape`],bestFor:[`vineyards and orchards`,`warm days and cool nights`,`water and soil questions`],seasonHint:`Late August to October for harvest. Spring bloom, in April and May, is the other good time and shows where the frost risk lies.`},{id:`appalachian-hollows-and-thermal-belts`,title:`Appalachian Hollows and Thermal Belts`,subtitle:`Cold valley floors and milder slopes`,description:`Southern and central Appalachian towns where warm slopes and cold hollows lie a few kilometres apart. Burkes Garden and Canaan Valley are high basins that collect cold air at night. Black Mountain sits on a slope above the frost. Highlands is among the rainiest places in the East. The regional forecast tells you little here; the position on the hill tells you more.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`black-mountain-nc`,`asheville-nc`,`boone-nc`,`highlands-nc`,`gatlinburg-tn`,`burkes-garden-va`,`canaan-valley-wv`,`mentone-al`],archetypeFilters:[`thermal-belt`,`cold-air-pool`,`cloud-forest`,`frost-hollow`],bestFor:[`frost hollows and warm slopes`,`cove forests`,`cool summers in the Southeast`],seasonHint:`Spring bloom, summer and autumn colour all work. Go out early on a clear morning to feel the cold in the hollows, then climb to the slopes.`},{id:`great-lakes-islands-and-snowbelts`,title:`Great Lakes Shores and Snowbelts`,subtitle:`What a large lake does to its shore`,description:`Fruit-growing shores kept mild by the lakes, the cool south shore of Lake Superior, and towns that take the heaviest lake-effect snow. Which of these a town gets depends on which shore it stands on and which way the wind blows across the water, so start each stop by finding both.`,tone:`ice`,placeIds:[`traverse-city-mi`,`houghton-mi`,`marquette-mi`,`grand-marais-mi`,`duluth-mn`,`erie-pa`,`geneva-on-the-lake-oh`,`leamington-on`,`prince-edward-co-on`],archetypeFilters:[`lake-moderated`,`lake-effect-snowbelt`,`orchard-valley`],bestFor:[`lake-effect snow`,`cool summer shores`,`orchards and vineyards`],seasonHint:`Summer shows how the lakes cool the shore. Late November to January shows lake-effect snow, before the lakes ice over.`},{id:`volcanic-soils-and-growing-zones`,title:`Volcanic Soils and What Grows on Them`,subtitle:`Lava, ash and the crops they carry`,description:`Places on volcanic ground, where the soil helps explain what grows: rainforest on young lava at Hilo, blue agave on the red soils around Tequila, coffee on the slopes at Coatepec and Xalapa, ponderosa pine on the cinder fields around Flagstaff. The soil notes are general. Dig a hole or ask a local grower before relying on them.`,tone:`ember`,placeIds:[`hilo-hi`,`tequila-mx`,`patzcuaro-mx`,`xalapa-mx`,`coatepec-mx`,`orizaba-mx`,`santa-fe-nm`,`flagstaff-az`],archetypeFilters:[`volcanic-upland`,`cloud-forest`,`eternal-spring-highland`],bestFor:[`soil and crops`,`gardening at altitude`,`coffee and agave country`],seasonHint:`Go in the growing season, when crops and gardens are in leaf and you can see how wet the soil stays.`},{id:`hidden-mountain-towns`,title:`Small Mountain Towns`,subtitle:`Little-visited bases with strongly local climates`,description:`Eight small towns, from Winthrop at 520 m to Real de Catorce at 2756 m. Several are cold: winter nights average −20°C at Crested Butte and −18°C at Stanley. Shops and medical care are limited, and the nearest large airport can be hours away. Use each town as a base and compare the valley floor with the slopes around it.`,tone:`sage`,placeIds:[`crested-butte-co`,`joseph-or`,`stanley-id`,`real-catorce-mx`,`cloudcroft-nm`,`silver-city-nm`,`winthrop-wa`,`nelson-bc`],archetypeFilters:[`sky-island-refuge`,`cold-air-pool`,`rain-shadow-sanctuary`,`alpine-tundra`],bestFor:[`quiet bases`,`cold, clear winters`,`exploring on foot`],seasonHint:`Late spring and early autumn have open roads, mild days and few visitors. Check for seasonal road closures in winter.`},{id:`cold-air-pools-and-frost-hollows`,title:`Cold-Air Pools and Frost Hollows`,subtitle:`Valley floors that run colder than expected`,description:`Basins where cold air collects at night. Go out at dawn on a clear, calm morning. The valley floor is at its coldest, a layer of fog or smoke marks the top of the cold air, and a short climb up the slope brings you into warmer air. The same pooling shortens the growing season, which matters if you plan to garden.`,tone:`ice`,placeIds:[`gunnison-co`,`logan-ut`,`canaan-valley-wv`,`burkes-garden-va`,`stanley-id`,`truckee-ca`,`fairbanks-ak`,`international-falls-mn`],archetypeFilters:[`cold-air-pool`,`frost-hollow`,`basin-inversion`],bestFor:[`frost pockets`,`winter inversions`,`checking a garden site`],seasonHint:`Clear, calm mornings in winter, early spring or autumn show the cold pool best.`}],a=Object.fromEntries(i.map(e=>[e.id,e])),o=[{id:`microclimate`,term:`Microclimate`,short:`A small area whose weather differs from the region around it because of local terrain, water or ground cover.`,long:`A microclimate can be a hillside, a canyon mouth, a lake shore or a whole valley. Inside it the temperature, rain, wind or humidity departs from what the latitude and the regional forecast would suggest. Every place in this atlas was chosen because it has one: Sequim gets about an eighth of the rain that falls in the Hoh valley on the far side of the Olympic Mountains, and Gunnison's valley floor is far colder on winter nights than the slopes above it.`,mechanism:`The regional climate sets the background. Mountains, water bodies, ground cover and night-time drainage of cold air change it locally.`,exampleIds:[`huachuca-az`,`sequim-wa`,`gunnison-co`]},{id:`station-representativeness`,term:`Station vs. hillside`,short:`Official weather records often come from airports and valley floors, which can differ from the slope where people live.`,long:`Weather stations are sited on flat, open ground, often at airports, because that suits aviation and gives consistent readings. A town a few kilometres away on a slope, under trees or in a hollow can have different frost dates, afternoon highs and humidity. Terraclima checks its gridded climate data against nearby stations and names the station when it applies a correction, so you can judge how well the numbers fit the spot you care about.`,mechanism:`Height and ground cover matter more than distance. A few hundred metres of elevation, or the edge of a forest, can change the record more than the gap between two airports does.`,exampleIds:[`huachuca-az`,`gunnison-co`,`black-mountain-nc`]},{id:`phytoclimate`,term:`Forest canopy climate (phytoclimate)`,short:`Under dense forest the air is cooler by day, milder at night, damper and calmer than in the open nearby.`,long:`A closed canopy blocks most direct sun, slows the wind and releases water vapour through its leaves. On a summer afternoon the forest floor can be several degrees cooler and noticeably more humid than a clearing a short walk away, and at night the canopy holds some warmth in. The effect is strongest in old-growth forest and in fog belts, where needles also comb water out of passing fog and drip it to the ground.`,mechanism:`Less sunlight reaches the ground, leaves evaporate water, and trunks and branches slow the wind. Together these lower the daytime high and keep the air moist.`,exampleIds:[`forks-wa`,`eureka-ca`,`haida-gwaii-bc`]},{id:`rain-shadow`,term:`Rain shadow`,short:`The dry side of a mountain range, downwind of where the storms drop their rain.`,long:`Moist air pushed up the windward side of a range cools, forms cloud and drops most of its rain or snow there. The air that comes down the far side holds less water and warms as it sinks, so clouds thin and the ground stays dry. You notice it as a change of landscape over a short drive: wet forest on one side of the pass, open pine, grass or sagebrush on the other, and more sun.`,mechanism:`Air cools as it is lifted over the range and its moisture condenses and falls. On the way down the air is compressed, warms and dries.`,exampleIds:[`sequim-wa`,`bishop-ca`,`osoyoos-bc`]},{id:`sky-island`,term:`Sky island`,short:`An isolated mountain range that is cool and forested on top and surrounded by desert or dry grassland.`,long:`Sky islands rise a kilometre or more above hot, dry lowlands. The air cools with height and the peaks catch more rain, so a drive up the mountain passes from desert scrub through oak woodland to pine and sometimes fir. The forest is cut off from other forests by the desert between ranges, which is why these mountains hold plants and animals found nowhere else.`,mechanism:`Temperature falls with elevation (about 6.5°C per 1000 m on average), and the mountain forces passing air upward so that it sheds rain on the heights.`,exampleIds:[`portal-az`,`huachuca-az`,`fort-davis-tx`]},{id:`lapse-rate`,term:`Lapse rate`,short:`The rate at which air gets colder as you climb: about 6.5°C per 1000 m on average.`,long:`Air pressure falls with height, and air that rises expands and cools. On average the atmosphere cools by about 6.5°C per 1000 m of height. Rising dry air cools faster, close to 9.8°C per 1000 m. Rising cloudy air cools more slowly, around 5.5°C per 1000 m, because condensing water releases heat. This is why a town at 2500 m is usually around 13°C cooler than a valley at 500 m an hour's drive away.`,mechanism:`Lower pressure aloft lets rising air expand. Expanding air does work on its surroundings and cools.`},{id:`cold-air-pooling`,term:`Cold-air pooling`,short:`On clear, calm nights cold air drains downhill and collects in valley bottoms and basins.`,long:`After sunset the ground loses heat to the sky and chills the air touching it. Cold air is heavier, so it slides downslope and gathers in the lowest ground, much as water would. By dawn a valley floor can be several degrees colder than the hillside 100 m above it, and in enclosed mountain basins it can be more than 10°C colder. You notice it as frost in the bottomland while the slopes are clear, fog lying in the hollows, and a shorter growing season on the valley floor.`,mechanism:`The ground cools by radiating heat on clear nights. The chilled air next to it becomes denser and flows to the lowest point, where it stays until sun or wind mixes it out.`,exampleIds:[`gunnison-co`,`logan-ut`,`canaan-valley-wv`,`truckee-ca`,`stanley-id`,`fairbanks-ak`]},{id:`gap-wind`,term:`Gap wind / gorge wind`,short:`A strong, steady wind through a low gap in a mountain range, driven by a pressure difference between the two sides.`,long:`When air pressure is higher on one side of a range than the other, air takes the lowest route across: a river gorge, a strait or a pass. The Columbia River Gorge, the Strait of Juan de Fuca and Crowsnest Pass all work this way. The wind blows from the same direction for days, and it is usually strongest where the gap opens out on the low-pressure side. Gaps also mark sharp climate boundaries, because they are where one region's air leaks into the next.`,mechanism:`The pressure difference pushes air along the gap and the walls keep it from spreading sideways, so it accelerates all the way through and peaks near the exit.`,exampleIds:[`hood-river-gorge`,`ellensburg-wa`,`pincher-creek-ab`,`la-ventosa-mx`]},{id:`santa-ana`,term:`Santa Ana and sundowner winds`,short:`Hot, very dry winds that blow from the land toward the coast in southern California, mostly in autumn and winter.`,long:`Santa Anas start when high pressure builds over the Great Basin. Air spills southwest through the mountain passes, sinks toward the coast and warms and dries as it descends. The temperature can jump 10°C in a few hours while humidity falls below 10%. Sundowners are a local cousin: evening winds that drop down the south face of the Santa Ynez Mountains onto the Santa Barbara coast. Both arrive when the vegetation is driest, and many of California's most destructive wildfires have burned under them.`,mechanism:`High pressure inland pushes air downslope toward the sea. Sinking air is compressed and heats up, and canyons and passes speed it up.`,exampleIds:[`ojai-ca`,`santa-barbara-ca`]},{id:`katabatic`,term:`Katabatic (drainage) wind`,short:`Cold, heavy air flowing downhill off glaciers, snowfields and high ground.`,long:`Air resting on ice or snow, or on high ground on a clear night, is chilled and becomes denser than the air around it. Gravity pulls it downslope. On an ordinary hillside this is a gentle evening breeze. Below a large icefield or at the head of a fjord it can be a strong, bitterly cold wind that blows for days and keeps the valley mouth colder than the coast on either side.`,mechanism:`Cooling from below makes the air denser, and gravity moves it down the slope. The bigger and colder the source area, the stronger the flow.`},{id:`hyper-maritime`,term:`Hyper-maritime climate`,short:`An outer-coast climate so controlled by the ocean that the warmest and coldest months differ by only about 6–12°C.`,long:`On exposed headlands and outer islands of the Pacific coast, the sea warms the winter air and cools the summer air. The difference between the average January and the average August can be smaller than the day-to-night swing in a dry inland valley. Summers stay cool, hard frost is uncommon, and cloud, wind and rain or fog are frequent. Heat waves rarely reach these coasts.`,mechanism:`Water stores far more heat than land and changes temperature slowly. Where the wind blows off the ocean nearly all year, the air takes on the sea's narrow range.`,exampleIds:[`eureka-ca`,`tofino-bc`,`prince-rupert-bc`,`forks-wa`,`sitka-ak`,`haida-gwaii-bc`,`cannon-beach-or`]},{id:`subarctic-continentality`,term:`Subarctic continental climate`,short:`The far-northern interior, where long, severe winters and short, warm summers put the coldest and warmest months 35–40°C apart.`,long:`Far from any ocean and at high latitude, nothing moderates the seasons. Winter is long and dark, and in the Yukon, the Northwest Territories and interior Alaska most winters bring nights near −40°C. Summer is brief but properly warm, with afternoons near 20°C and daylight most of the night. Only one to three months average above 10°C, which is the usual definition of subarctic. Permafrost, spruce forest and a short, intense growing season follow from that.`,mechanism:`Land heats and cools quickly, and at these latitudes the amount of sunlight changes enormously between December and June.`,exampleIds:[`whitehorse-yt`,`yellowknife-nt`,`fairbanks-ak`,`dawson-city-yt`,`inuvik-nt`]},{id:`inversion`,term:`Temperature inversion`,short:`A layer of warmer air above colder air, which stops the air below from mixing upward.`,long:`Air normally gets colder with height. Under calm high pressure, especially on winter nights, the lowest layer can end up colder than the air above it. The warm layer acts as a lid: cold air, fog, wood smoke and exhaust stay in the valley, sometimes for days. From a ridge you see it as a flat grey deck below you while you stand in sunshine.`,mechanism:`The ground cools the air near it at night, and sinking air under high pressure warms the layer above. Cold air under warm air is stable, so it does not rise.`},{id:`aspect`,term:`Aspect and slope`,short:`Which way a slope faces, and how steep it is, decides how much sun it gets.`,long:`In the Northern Hemisphere a south- or southwest-facing slope meets the sun more directly, so it runs warmer and drier and loses its snow earlier. A north-facing slope across the same valley stays cooler, holds snow and soil moisture longer, and often carries denser forest. The difference can be as large as moving a few hundred kilometres north or south, and you can see it in which side of a valley has trees.`,mechanism:`A surface tilted toward the sun catches more sunlight on the same area of ground than one tilted away.`},{id:`orographic-lift`,term:`Orographic lift`,short:`Air forced to rise over mountains cools, forms cloud and drops rain or snow.`,long:`Wind that meets a mountain range has to go up. As it rises it cools until its water vapour condenses, which is why the windward sides of ranges are wet and why a cap of cloud can sit on a summit on an otherwise clear day. The same process makes mountain snowpacks and, on the far side, rain shadows.`,mechanism:`Rising air expands and cools. Cooler air holds less water vapour, so the excess condenses into cloud and precipitation.`},{id:`marine-layer`,term:`Marine layer`,short:`A shallow layer of cool, damp ocean air that brings low cloud and fog to the coast.`,long:`Where the sea is cold, the air just above it is chilled and stays trapped under warmer air higher up. Low grey cloud forms in that layer, moves inland at night and usually burns back to the beach by afternoon. Under it, summer afternoons sit near 20°C while valleys an hour inland pass 35°C. On the California coast this is the “June gloom” of early summer.`,mechanism:`Cold water cools the air from below, and warm, sinking air above forms an inversion that caps it. Cloud fills the cool layer.`,exampleIds:[`monterey-ca`,`santa-cruz-felton-ca`,`point-reyes-ca`]},{id:`thermal-belt`,term:`Thermal belt`,short:`A band partway up a slope that stays warmer on frosty nights than the valley floor below it.`,long:`On clear, calm nights cold air drains off the slopes and pools in the valley. The hillside above the pool sits in the warmer air of the inversion, so it can be several degrees milder at dawn than the bottomland. Higher still, the air turns colder again with altitude and the wind is stronger. Fruit growers have long planted orchards and vineyards on these mid-slopes, where spring frost comes less often and the frost-free season can be weeks longer.`,mechanism:`Cold air drains away downslope and is replaced by warmer air from the inversion layer above the valley's cold pool.`,exampleIds:[`black-mountain-nc`,`hood-river-or`]},{id:`chinook`,term:`Chinook / foehn`,short:`A warm, dry wind blowing down the lee side of a mountain range, able to raise winter temperatures by 20°C or more in hours.`,long:`Air crossing a range drops rain or snow on the windward side, then descends the far side and warms as it is compressed. It arrives at the foot of the mountains warm, dry and often very strong. East of the Rockies a chinook can take a town from deep cold to well above freezing in an afternoon and strip the snow cover in a day. From the plains you can see it coming as a clear arch of sky under a band of cloud along the mountains. “Foehn” is the name for the same wind in the Alps.`,mechanism:`Sinking air warms by about 9.8°C per 1000 m of descent. Heat released when moisture condensed on the windward side adds to the warming.`,exampleIds:[`lethbridge-ab`,`pincher-creek-ab`,`boulder-co`,`spearfish-sd`]},{id:`lake-effect`,term:`Lake effect`,short:`A large lake keeps its shores milder, and in early winter cold air crossing open water produces heavy snow downwind.`,long:`Water warms and cools more slowly than land. Beside a big lake, spring comes late, autumn lingers and the first hard frosts are delayed, which is what makes the fruit belts along Lake Michigan, Lake Erie and Lake Ontario possible. In late autumn and winter, cold air blowing over the still-unfrozen lake picks up heat and moisture and drops it as snow in narrow bands on the downwind shore. A town in a band can get a metre of snow while one 30 km away gets a dusting. The snow eases once the lake freezes over.`,mechanism:`Heat and water vapour pass from the warm lake into much colder air above it. The warmed air rises, forms cloud and snows where it meets the shore and higher ground.`,exampleIds:[`traverse-city-mi`,`niagara-on-the-lake`,`redfield-ny`]},{id:`upwelling`,term:`Coastal upwelling`,short:`Wind pushes surface water away from the coast and cold water rises from below to replace it, chilling the shore.`,long:`Along the Pacific coast in spring and summer, northwest winds blow down the coastline. The Earth's rotation turns the moving surface water offshore, and cold, nutrient-rich water wells up in its place. The sea off northern California can be 10–13°C in July. That cold water keeps coastal air cool and feeds the summer fog, and it supports rich fisheries. Eureka's summer afternoons average about 18°C while the Central Valley is above 35°C.`,mechanism:`Wind along the coast, turned by the Earth's rotation, moves surface water out to sea (Ekman transport). Deeper, colder water rises to fill the gap.`,exampleIds:[`monterey-ca`,`eureka-ca`,`ensenada-mx`]},{id:`continentality`,term:`Continentality`,short:`How far a place's climate is shaped by distance from the sea: the farther inland, the bigger the swings.`,long:`Oceans store heat and release it slowly, so coasts have small differences between summer and winter and between day and night. Land heats and cools quickly, so the interior of a continent has hot summers, cold winters and drier air. Tofino and Medicine Hat lie at nearly the same latitude; their Januaries and Julys are nothing alike.`,exampleIds:[`tofino-bc`,`medicine-hat-ab`]},{id:`hardiness-zone`,term:`Hardiness zone`,short:`A rating of how cold winter gets, based on the coldest night of an average year, used to judge which plants survive.`,long:`The USDA system averages the lowest temperature of each year over 30 years and sorts places into numbered zones, each a 10-degree-Fahrenheit band split into “a” and “b” halves. A higher number means a milder winter. Canada publishes its own zones, which also take account of summer warmth, snow cover and wind. A zone tells you whether a perennial will survive the winter. It says nothing about summer heat, rainfall or the date of the last frost, which matter just as much.`},{id:`chill-hours`,term:`Chill hours`,short:`The hours of winter cold, roughly between 0 and 7°C, that fruit trees need before they will bloom properly.`,long:`Apples, pears, peaches and cherries rest through winter and need a certain amount of cool weather before their buds will open normally. With too little chill, bloom is late, ragged and light. A variety that needs little chill, planted where winters are long, is satisfied early and may flower in the first warm spell, only to be caught by a later frost. Growers match the variety to the local chill total.`},{id:`soil-drainage`,term:`Soil drainage`,short:`How quickly water moves down through a soil.`,long:`Drainage depends on how much sand, silt and clay the soil contains, how it is structured, and what lies beneath it. Sandy soil drains fast, dries out and loses nutrients. Heavy clay or a hard layer below holds water, and roots rot. Most garden plants do best in between, in soil that stays moist but never waterlogged.`},{id:`karst`,term:`Karst`,short:`Limestone country where rain sinks underground through cracks and caves instead of running off in streams.`,long:`Slightly acidic rainwater slowly dissolves limestone, leaving sinkholes, caves and underground rivers. There are few surface streams, and the water comes back out at large springs, often cold and clear. Soils are thin, alkaline and quick to dry. Sinkholes and cave mouths stay cool and damp, so a karst landscape holds small humid pockets within dry uplands.`},{id:`monsoon-edge`,term:`Monsoon edge`,short:`The outer limit of the summer monsoon, where the rains arrive in some years and fall short in others.`,long:`The North American Monsoon brings afternoon thunderstorms to northwest Mexico and the US Southwest from July to September. Its core, over the Sierra Madre Occidental, is reliable. Toward the northern and western fringe the moisture arrives in bursts, and a wet summer can be followed by a nearly dry one. Places on the edge have a long dry spring, a sudden green-up when the storms begin, and large differences from year to year.`,exampleIds:[`silver-city-nm`,`alamos-mx`]},{id:`climate-normals`,term:`Climate normals`,short:`Thirty-year averages of temperature, rain and other measures, used to describe a place's usual climate.`,long:`A single year can be unusual, so climate is described by averages over 30 years. The World Meteorological Organization's current standard period is 1991–2020, and it is updated every ten years. Terraclima's default figures cover 1996–2025 and it shows 1991–2020 for comparison. A normal describes what has been typical. It is not a forecast, and with the climate warming, each new period runs a little warmer than the last.`},{id:`scenario-uncertainty`,term:`Scenario uncertainty`,short:`Projections of future climate depend on how much greenhouse gas the world emits, so each one is tied to an emissions scenario.`,long:`Climate models are run under several emissions pathways, called SSPs: SSP1-2.6 assumes rapid cuts, SSP2-4.5 a middle course and SSP5-8.5 very high emissions. Up to about 2050 the scenarios give similar results, because much of that warming is already under way. After mid-century they diverge widely. Any projection should say which scenario it uses.`},{id:`diurnal-range`,term:`Diurnal temperature range`,short:`The difference between the afternoon high and the overnight low on a typical day.`,long:`Dry air, clear skies and high elevation let the ground heat quickly by day and lose that heat quickly at night, so high deserts swing by 15–25°C between afternoon and dawn. Humid or cloudy coasts swing by only 5–8°C. A wide range means cool nights for sleeping after hot days, a higher risk of late frost, and better sugar and acid balance in wine grapes and tree fruit.`,mechanism:`Water vapour and cloud trap outgoing heat at night. Where the air is dry and clear, and the ground is dry, nights cool fast and days heat fast.`,exampleIds:[`wenatchee-wa`,`bishop-ca`,`marfa-tx`]},{id:`dew-point`,term:`Dew point`,short:`The temperature to which air must cool for dew to form; the most direct measure of how humid the air feels.`,long:`Relative humidity rises and falls through the day as the temperature changes, even when the amount of moisture in the air stays the same. Dew point measures the moisture itself. Below about 10°C the air feels dry; 10–15°C is comfortable; 16–20°C feels sticky; above 21°C it is oppressive and nights give little relief. Dew point is the main reason a 30°C afternoon in Santa Fe and one in New Orleans feel so different.`},{id:`fire-weather`,term:`Fire weather`,short:`Hot, dry, windy conditions in which a fire starts easily and spreads fast.`,long:`Three things together make a dangerous fire day: very low humidity, strong wind, and vegetation already dried by heat or drought. There is no single definition, but a US red-flag warning typically calls for humidity at or below about 15% and sustained winds of 40 km/h or more for several hours. Santa Ana winds, chinooks and gap winds produce these conditions over wide areas at once, and they often come in autumn, when fuels are driest.`},{id:`monsoon`,term:`North American Monsoon`,short:`A summer shift in the winds that brings thunderstorms to northwest Mexico and the US Southwest from about July to September.`,long:`In early summer the Mexican Plateau and the desert Southwest heat up, and the dry westerly winds of spring give way to flow from the south and southeast. That flow carries moisture from the Gulf of California, the eastern Pacific and the Gulf of Mexico. Storms build over the mountains most afternoons and drift over the valleys by evening. The season supplies a third to a half of the year's rain in Arizona and New Mexico, and as much as three-quarters in Sonora. On the ground it means a hot, dry June, then towering clouds by noon, short violent downpours, flash floods in dry washes, and hills that turn green within weeks.`,mechanism:`Strong summer heating of the high ground shifts the high-level wind pattern north and draws tropical moisture inland. Mountains and afternoon heat trigger the storms.`,exampleIds:[`silver-city-nm`,`flagstaff-az`,`alamos-mx`]},{id:`radiative-cooling`,term:`Radiative cooling`,short:`The ground losing heat to the night sky, fastest when the air is clear, dry and still.`,long:`The Earth's surface gives off heat as infrared radiation all the time. Cloud and humid air send much of it back. On a clear, dry, calm night little comes back, so the ground and the air just above it cool quickly. By dawn they have often cooled by 15°C or more from the afternoon high. Frost, dew, valley fog and cold-air pooling all start this way.`},{id:`urban-heat-island`,term:`Urban heat island`,short:`Cities run warmer than the countryside around them, most of all on calm, clear nights.`,long:`Pavement, brick and concrete absorb heat through the day and release it slowly after dark, and there are fewer trees and less moist soil to cool the air. Averaged over a year a city centre is typically 1–3°C warmer than nearby farmland, and on a still summer night it can be 5–10°C warmer. Warm nights are the part that matters most for health in a heat wave. Parks, river corridors and leafy neighbourhoods inside the same city can be noticeably cooler.`},{id:`hurricane-coast`,term:`Hurricane coast`,short:`A coast where tropical storms and hurricanes make landfall often enough to shape how people build and insure.`,long:`The Gulf of Mexico, the Atlantic seaboard, the Caribbean side of the Yucatán and Mexico's Pacific coast all lie in the path of tropical cyclones, mostly between June and November. The average weather on these coasts can be pleasant for most of the year. The risk is the rare storm: surge, flooding rain and wind that can undo decades of building in a day. Elevation above sea level and distance from open water matter more here than the monthly averages do.`},{id:`tropical-isothermal`,term:`Tropical isothermal climate`,short:`A tropical climate in which monthly temperatures hardly change through the year.`,long:`Near the tropics the midday sun is high in every season and day length changes little, so there is no real winter or summer. In the Mexican highlands the warmest and coldest months are usually only 3–8°C apart. The seasons are a rainy one and a dry one. Altitude sets the level: the same latitude is hot at the coast and mild at 2000 m, which is where the “eternal spring” reputation of the highland cities comes from.`,exampleIds:[`oaxaca-mx`,`san-cristobal-mx`,`patzcuaro-mx`]},{id:`eo-reference-sensors`,term:`Satellite imagery (how the atlas uses it)`,short:`Sentinel-2 and Landsat are the two public satellite programmes most used to study vegetation, snow, moisture and surface heat. Terraclima refers to them but does not display their images.`,long:`Sentinel-2 photographs the land in visible and infrared light at 10 to 20 m detail every few days. Landsat works at 30 m, also measures surface temperature, and has a record going back to the 1970s. A place profile notes which kinds of satellite measurement would be informative there, and where frequent cloud, snow or coastal fog would leave few clear images. These notes are derived from the place's climate and terrain data. They are not live imagery.`,mechanism:`Sentinel-2 gives finer detail of land cover. Landsat adds thermal bands and a much longer history.`,exampleIds:[`portal-az`,`sequim-wa`,`hood-river-gorge`]},{id:`relief-texture-proxy`,term:`Relief texture (atlas estimate)`,short:`A rough score for how much the fine detail of the terrain is likely to matter to a place's local climate.`,long:`In canyons, sky islands, karst, fog belts and at the sharp edge of a rain shadow, conditions change over distances too small for coarse elevation maps to show. The relief-texture score flags those places. It is estimated from elevation, the size of the seasonal temperature swing, the seasonal change in humidity and the place's terrain types. Treat it as a prompt to look at detailed topography, not as a measurement.`,exampleIds:[`portal-az`,`gunnison-co`,`forks-wa`]},{id:`lidar-field-practice`,term:`Lidar`,short:`Laser scanning from aircraft that maps the ground surface and the height of the forest above it in fine detail.`,long:`A lidar instrument fires laser pulses downward and times their return. Some pulses reflect from treetops and some reach the ground through gaps, so one survey yields both the bare ground under the forest and the height of the canopy. Hydrologists and fire crews use it to map channels, terraces, levees and fuel loads that ordinary elevation maps smooth away. Terraclima does not include lidar data. Its relief-texture score points to places where a lidar map would add the most.`,exampleIds:[`canaan-valley-wv`,`silver-city-nm`,`haida-gwaii-bc`]},{id:`koppen`,term:`Köppen climate classification`,short:`A system of two- or three-letter codes that sorts climates by temperature and by when the rain falls.`,long:`The first letter gives the broad group: A tropical, B dry, C temperate, D cold-winter continental, E polar. The second letter usually describes rainfall: f for rain in every season, s for a dry summer, w for a dry winter. A third letter grades summer warmth, from a (hot) to c (cool, short). So Csb, as in Sequim or Victoria, means temperate with a dry, warm summer, and Dfc, as in Fairbanks, means cold winters, moisture all year and a short, cool summer. The code is a broad label. Two towns with the same code can feel quite different, which is what the rest of each profile describes.`,mechanism:`The classes come from fixed thresholds applied to monthly average temperature and precipitation. Terraclima follows the thresholds in Beck et al. (2018).`,exampleIds:[`sequim-wa`,`fairbanks-ak`,`merida-mx`]},{id:`growing-degree-days`,term:`Growing degree days`,short:`A running total of warmth over the season, used to judge which crops have time to ripen.`,long:`For each day, take the average temperature and subtract a base below which the crop does not grow; Terraclima uses 10°C. Days colder than the base count as zero. Add up the year and you have the growing degree days. A long frost-free season does not guarantee a high total. Eureka goes about 315 days a year without frost, yet it gathers well under half the warmth that Napa does, too little for melons or most wine grapes.`,exampleIds:[`napa-ca`,`eureka-ca`,`osoyoos-bc`]},{id:`heat-index`,term:`Heat index`,short:`What a hot afternoon feels like in the shade once humidity is taken into account.`,long:`The body cools itself by evaporating sweat, and humid air slows evaporation. The heat index combines air temperature and humidity into the temperature that would feel the same at ordinary humidity. At 32°C with 70% humidity it is about 41°C. In very dry air it reads slightly below the thermometer. Terraclima uses the US National Weather Service formula with the humidity of the afternoon, when the day's high occurs, and not the daily average, which would overstate it. The index assumes shade and a light breeze; full sun feels hotter.`,exampleIds:[`new-orleans-la`,`austin-tx`,`yuma-az`]},{id:`wet-bulb`,term:`Wet-bulb temperature`,short:`The lowest temperature that evaporation alone can cool something to; a measure of how hard it is for the body to shed heat.`,long:`Wrap a thermometer bulb in wet cloth and swing it through the air: evaporation cools it to the wet-bulb temperature. In dry air that is far below the air temperature, which is why sweat and evaporative coolers work well in the desert. In humid air it is close to the air temperature and sweating helps little. Sustained wet-bulb readings in the high 20s°C are dangerous for people working outdoors, and around 31–35°C even a resting, healthy person cannot keep cool. Terraclima estimates it from the afternoon high and afternoon humidity.`,exampleIds:[`merida-mx`,`new-orleans-la`,`death-valley-ca`]},{id:`de-martonne-aridity`,term:`De Martonne aridity index`,short:`Annual precipitation in millimetres divided by the mean annual temperature in degrees Celsius plus 10. Higher means wetter.`,long:`Emmanuel de Martonne proposed this index in 1926. Dividing rainfall by temperature allows for the fact that a warm place needs more rain than a cool one to stay equally moist. The bands used here are: hyperarid (under 5), arid (5–10), semi-arid (10–20), Mediterranean (20–24), sub-humid (24–28), humid (28–35), very humid (35–55) and extremely humid (55 and over). The index breaks down where the mean annual temperature is near −10°C, so Terraclima shows no value for the coldest places.`,mechanism:`It weighs water supply against warmth using annual totals only, so it cannot tell a dry-summer climate from a dry-winter one.`,exampleIds:[`sequim-wa`,`yuma-az`,`hilo-hi`]},{id:`conrad-continentality`,term:`Conrad continentality index`,short:`A 0-to-100 scale of how continental a climate is, from the gap between the warmest and coldest months, adjusted for latitude.`,long:`Victor Conrad's 1946 formula is K = 1.7 × A ÷ sin(latitude + 10°) − 14, where A is the difference between the warmest and coldest monthly mean temperatures in degrees Celsius. Seasonal swings grow toward the poles even over the ocean, and the latitude term removes that effect so that what remains reflects distance from the sea. Values near 0 are fully oceanic and values near 100 match the most extreme interior of Siberia. Small-range tropical coasts can score below zero. Terraclima's bands run from extreme oceanic to extreme continental.`,mechanism:`A large annual range at a low latitude scores as more continental than the same range near the Arctic.`,exampleIds:[`hilo-hi`,`monterey-ca`,`fairbanks-ak`]},{id:`thornthwaite-pet`,term:`Thornthwaite PET`,short:`An estimate, from temperature and day length alone, of how much water the air could evaporate from well-watered ground.`,long:`Potential evapotranspiration (PET) is the amount of water that soil and plants would give up to the atmosphere if water were never short. C. W. Thornthwaite's 1948 method estimates it from monthly mean temperature and hours of daylight, which makes it usable wherever a thermometer record exists. Because it ignores humidity, wind and sunshine, it tends to read low in dry, sunny, windy climates and high in humid ones. Terraclima reports annual PET as a depth of water, so it can be set directly against annual precipitation.`,mechanism:`A yearly heat index I is the sum of (T/5)^1.514 over months above freezing. Monthly PET in millimetres is 16 × (10T/I)^a, with T in degrees Celsius, scaled by day length and the number of days in the month.`,exampleIds:[`sequim-wa`,`yuma-az`,`hilo-hi`]},{id:`selianinov-htc`,term:`Selianinov hydrothermal coefficient (HTC)`,short:`Growing-season rain divided by growing-season warmth: a measure of whether crops get enough water during the months they grow.`,long:`G. T. Selianinov's coefficient, from 1928, is 10 times the rain that falls in the months averaging above 10°C, divided by the sum of daily mean temperatures over those months. Below 0.4 is very dry, 1.0–1.3 is adequately moist and above 1.6 is wet. Terraclima approximates the daily sum as each month's mean temperature times its number of days. Because it looks only at the warm months, the index can call a place dry in the season that matters to a farmer even when the yearly rainfall looks adequate.`,mechanism:`Winter rain is left out. Sequim gets about 430 mm of rain a year, most of it from October to March, and is dry by this measure during its growing season.`,exampleIds:[`sequim-wa`,`hilo-hi`,`osoyoos-bc`]},{id:`unep-aridity`,term:`UNEP aridity index (P/PET)`,short:`Annual precipitation divided by annual potential evapotranspiration. Below 0.65 counts as dryland.`,long:`The UN Environment Programme's 1992 World Atlas of Desertification set this ratio as the standard measure of aridity: hyperarid (under 0.05), arid (0.05–0.20), semi-arid (0.20–0.50), dry sub-humid (0.50–0.65) and humid (0.65 and over). The top of the fraction is the water that arrives and the bottom is the water the air could take away, here estimated with Thornthwaite's method.`,mechanism:`A value above 1 means more rain falls in a year than the air could evaporate. Well below 1, soils are dry for most of the year unless irrigated.`,exampleIds:[`sequim-wa`,`yuma-az`,`hilo-hi`]}];export{r as a,n as i,i as n,e as o,a as r,t as s,o as t};